## DESCRIPTION
## Algebra, Exponential functions
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Algebra)
## DBchapter(Exponential and logarithmic expressions and functions)
## DBsection(Exponential functions)
## Date(06/16/2017)
## Institution(Colorado Community College System)
## Author(James Morski)
## Level(1)
## MO(1)
## KEYWORDS('algebra','exponential functions')

#  Initialization

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'parserPopUp.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric');

@x = (1 .. 4);
$a = random(10,  100, 10);
$b = random(0.3, 0.9, 0.1);
$f = Compute("$a * $b^x")->reduce;
@y = map { $f->eval(x => $_) } @x;

Context("Numeric");

$popup = DropDown([ 'linear', "exponential", "neither" ], "exponential");

#  Main text

BEGIN_PGML
Determine whether the table could represent a function that is linear, exponential, or neither.  If the function is exponential or linear, find a function that passes through the points.  If the function is neither exponential nor linear, type 'NONE'. (if exponential, write the form [`f(x)=ab^x`], if linear, write the form [`f(x)=mx+b`])

[#
    [.[`x`].] [.[$x[0]].][.[$x[1]].][.[$x[2]].][.[$x[3]].]*
    [.[`f(x)`].] [.[$y[0]].][.[$y[1]].][.[$y[2]].][.[$y[3]].]
#]{horizontalrules =>1, align => '|c|c|c|c|c|', padding => 0.3}


The function is [_]{$popup} 

An form of the function is [` f(x)= `] [_]{$f}{20} 

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_HINT
Look at how the values of [`f(x)`] are changing, are they changing by the same amount each time such as adding 5 each time? If so, that would be linear.

Or are they changing by the same factor each time such as cutting in half each time? If so, that would be exponential.
END_PGML_HINT

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
If the data changed the same amount for each change in unit [`x`], then the function would be linear, so let's check if it is exponential. 

[```\frac{f(2)}{f(1)} = \frac{[$y[1]]}{[$y[0]]} = [$b]```]
[```\frac{f(3)}{f(2)} =  \frac{[$y[2]]}{[$y[1]]} = [$b]```]
[```\frac{f(4)}{f(3)} = \frac{[$y[3]]}{[$y[2]]} = [$b]```]
Because it has the form that 
[```\frac{f(x+1)}{f(x)} = C```]
and because
[```\frac{f(x+1)}{f(x)} = \frac{a b^{x+1}}{a b^x} = b```]
then [` b= [$b]`].

To find [`a`], use any of the data or 
[``` f(1) = a \cdot b^1 = [$y[0]] ```] and solving for [`a`] or 
[``` a = \frac{[$y[0]]}{[$b]} = [$a] ```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

COMMENT('Uses PGML.');

ENDDOCUMENT();
